e('Deadlocks are timing bugs.','Many concurrency deadlocks depend on timing; others, such as recursive acquisition of a non-reentrant lock, happen deterministically.','Notallinterleavingdependent.')
e('usually under peak load, in production.','possibly under production load.','Unsupportedprevalence.')
e('| ~100% CPU, stacks change | Livelock or busy loop |','| High CPU, stacks repeat or change | Livelock, busy loop, or spinning deadlock |','SpindeadlocknotzeroCPU.')
e('| Blocked in I/O, not locks | Slow dependency |','| Blocked in I/O, not locks | Slow dependency or an I/O dependency cycle |','Pipes/RPCcandeadlock.')
e("A thread that's in the same lock call in every dump is genuinely stuck; one that's somewhere different each time is merely slow.","Repeated stacks are evidence of a persistent wait, not proof of deadlock; a legitimate long wait can look the same. Changing stacks can indicate useful progress or livelock. Confirm dependencies and application progress.",'Finiteobservationnotproof.')
e('`jstack <pid>` (or `jcmd <pid> Thread.print`, or `kill -3 <pid>`, which prints to the JVM\'s stdout) dumps every thread. The JVM also runs its own cycle detection over monitors and `java.util.concurrent` locks and prints the result at the end:','For platform threads, use `jstack -l <pid>` or `jcmd <pid> Thread.print -l`; on supported Unix HotSpot JVMs, `kill -3 <pid>` requests a dump to the JVM output. Classic dumps and management deadlock detection do not cover every virtual-thread dependency. The JVM can report current cycles involving object monitors and supported ownable synchronizers:','Virtualthreads unsupported;notalljucprimitives.',ja)
e('The same detection is available in-process through `ThreadMXBean.findDeadlockedThreads()`, which some services poll from a watchdog thread that logs and alerts.','`ThreadMXBean.findDeadlockedThreads()` exposes detection for platform threads using monitors and supported ownable synchronizers; it does not detect virtual-thread cycles. A watchdog can log and alert on reported cycles.','APIexactscope.',ja)
e('without stopping it.','without terminating it. Sampling may briefly pause threads; `--nonblocking` avoids pausing at the cost of potentially inconsistent samples.','Pyspydefaultpauses.',py)
e('if **every** goroutine is blocked, it crashes','if the scheduler finds no runnable work or supported source of future wakeups, it may crash','Timers/network/Cinterop exceptions.',go)
e('glibc stores the owning thread\'s id in the mutex, so you can ask who holds each one:','For ordinary glibc mutexes, an internal owner field can help identify the holder. It is not portable POSIX API; robust, PI, elision and transient states require implementation-specific interpretation:','Ownerfieldnotalltypesuniversal.')
e('and the thread state will be `S` (interruptible sleep).','often in an interruptible sleep state for ordinary userspace futex waits. Exact state and wait-channel visibility depend on the wait type, kernel and permissions.','NotallfutexwaitS.')
e('Kernel deadlocks usually leave tasks in state `D` (uninterruptible sleep). The **hung task detector** (`khungtaskd`) logs a warning with a stack trace for any task stuck in `D` for longer than `kernel.hung_task_timeout_secs`, typically 120 s.','Some kernel waits leave tasks in state `D` (uninterruptible sleep), while spinning deadlocks may instead cause lockup warnings. With the relevant configuration enabled, the **hung task detector** checks prolonged uninterruptible waits against `kernel.hung_task_timeout_secs` (commonly 120 s); settings, exclusions and reporting limits affect what is logged.','Dnotdeadlockdiagnosis,noteverytaskreport.',lk)
e('not production.','with deployment depending on overhead and diagnostic needs.','Notcategoricallyneverproduction.',ld)
e('Same-class nesting (locking a parent and a child inode) needs annotations such as `mutex_lock_nested()`, or it gives false positives.','Legitimate same-class nesting needs a real hierarchy and correctly mapped annotations such as `mutex_lock_nested()`. Incorrect annotations can hide genuine bugs as well as create false reports.','Annotationnotproof.',ld)
e('**ThreadSanitizer** (`-fsanitize=thread` in Clang and GCC) is best known for data races, but it also builds a lock-order graph and reports `lock-order-inversion (potential deadlock)` when it sees a cycle, with the stack where each edge was created. Detection is on by default (`detect_deadlocks=1`). Expect roughly a 5–15× slowdown and 5–10× more memory, so it belongs in CI and test runs.','**ThreadSanitizer** (`-fsanitize=thread`) primarily detects data races. Some runtimes also support an experimental lock-order detector for supported pthread primitives, reporting `lock-order-inversion (potential deadlock)`. Check the compiler/runtime and enable `TSAN_OPTIONS=detect_deadlocks=1`; do not assume all platforms, versions or custom locks support it. Clang documents typical overall TSan costs of 5–15× runtime and 5–10× memory; these are broad tool estimates, not a benchmark for this example.','Runtimecapabilities/defaultsnotportable.',ts)
e('// a.c: compile with -fsanitize=thread','// Fragment: include <pthread.h>.\n// Compile with -fsanitize=thread.','Missingheader/mainfragmentcontract.',ts)
e('Run `t1` to completion, *then* `t2`, and the program never hangs, but TSan reports the inversion `a => b => a`.','With the lock-order detector available and enabled, running `t1` to completion and then `t2` can report `a => b => a` without an actual deadlock. Both orders must be observed in the same instrumented process; separate executions do not share the graph.','Separate-runmisconception/supportqualification.',ts)
e('at a larger slowdown.','with workload-dependent overhead.','Unsupporteduniversalrelativebenchmark.',['https://valgrind.org/docs/manual/hg-manual.html'])
e('**TLA+** checks a design-level model of the protocol before you write code;','A model checker such as **TLC** checks a design written in **TLA+** before you write implementation code;','Languagevschecker.')
e('The rules below, roughly in order of how much trouble they save:','Useful rules include:','Unsupportedeffectivenessranking.')
e('**Hold one lock at a time.** Most deadlocks need two. Copy what you need, release, then act.','**Avoid nested lock acquisition.** Copy what you need, release, then act, while preserving data invariants. A single non-reentrant lock can still self-deadlock; communication and pool dependencies also matter.','Deadlocknotrequiretwolocks.')
e('A single thread or event loop that owns the data (Redis\'s command execution is single-threaded) needs no locks.','Exclusive ownership can remove locks around that data, though background I/O, shared state and re-entrant callbacks still require coordination. Redis largely serializes command execution while also using background work and optional I/O threads.','Singlethreadnotuniversallockfreearchitecture.')
e('and always retry on deadlock errors.','and use bounded, safe whole-transaction retries when appropriate. A timeout needs explicit cleanup; InnoDB normally leaves earlier transaction locks held after a statement timeout.','Blindalwaysretryunsafe.',pg+['https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-error-handling.html'])
e('> [!example] A post-mortem pattern','> [!example] An illustrative failure pattern','Notclaimedempiricalincident.')
e('- Confirm a deadlock first: near-zero CPU and identical stacks across several dumps a few seconds apart.','- Use multiple dumps and progress measurements to investigate; confirm a dependency cycle rather than diagnosing from CPU use alone.','Heuristicnotproof.')
e('- Go only detects the case where every goroutine is blocked; partial deadlocks need a goroutine dump.','- Go detects some global no-progress states; timers, network waits and runtime modes affect detection. Investigate partial deadlocks with dumps.','Allblocked notsufficient.',go)
e('- Lockdep and ThreadSanitizer record lock-order edges and report a potential deadlock the first time both orders are seen, even if the threads never collide.','- Lockdep and supported TSan lock-order detectors can report observed order cycles without an actual collision. Unobserved paths and unsupported primitives remain unchecked.','Capabilitybounded.',ts+ld)
f(1,'explanation','Restarting loses the live state, so collect evidence first when operationally feasible. Existing dumps, core files and detailed logs may preserve useful evidence after restart.','Stateonlyrunningclaimtooabsolute.',ja,o=0)
f(1,'workedExample','Near-zero CPU suggests waiting, which may be deadlock, a lost wake-up or a slow dependency. Capture several thread dumps and inspect current wait/ownership relations. Repeated stacks alone do not prove deadlock; changing stacks do not prove useful progress. Java can identify supported platform-thread monitor/synchronizer cycles. Preserve evidence before restarting when feasible.','Correctdiagnosticcertainty/virtualscope.',ja)
f(2,'text','The scheduler detects some global no-progress states; active HTTP/network work can keep a partial deadlock from triggering that check','Allblockednotsolecriterion.',go,o=0)
f(2,'explanation','Lock type alone does not determine detection. The scheduler considers runnable work and possible wakeups; some runtime modes also change its checks.','No unconditionalglobalassertion.',go,o=1)
f(2,'workedExample','Go\'s scheduler can report a global deadlock when no work can run and no relevant timer, network or other wakeup can make progress. A partial mutex cycle in a working HTTP server does not meet that condition. Runtime/cgo modes also affect detection. Inspect goroutine dumps or a configured pprof endpoint; SIGQUIT normally terminates the process after printing stacks.','Avoidallserversalways/alldeadlocks.',go)
f(3,'explanation','A snapshot at acquire does not prove the lock remains held: a released waiter may not yet have run, or another thread may have reacquired it. Multiple observations help establish the dependency.','Unlocknotinstantreturn.',py,o=1)
f(3,'workedExample','Python Lock has no specified owner and allows release by another thread. Stacks can help infer which code acquired it, especially disciplined with-blocks, but that is not an authoritative owner table. Inspect object identity and control flow, or add lock instrumentation. The glibc internal owner field is an implementation-specific aid, not a portable guarantee for every mutex kind.','Dontinfercertainownerstacks.',py)
